    "slug": "triad-the-flat-fifth",
    "title": "The Note That Hasn't Moved",
    "summary": "Drop the fifth a fret as well as the third and the chord turns diminished — and in the key of C there is exactly one of them, a dominant seventh chord with its root taken away.",
    "tags": ["triads", "chords", "diminished", "fretboard"],
    "readingTimeMin": 5,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 3 ended on a fact about what hadn't moved: the third did the work, and the fifth stayed exactly where major left it. This chapter moves the fifth. A diminished triad is degrees "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 b5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — over "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", that's "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", two minor thirds stacked on top of each other, "
        },
        {
          "text": "3+3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One more reminder, since a learner may be arriving after a gap: "
        },
        {
          "text": "3·5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " means string 3, fret 5. A chart is six slots, low E first, with "
        },
        {
          "text": "x",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " for a string that isn't played, and string 1 is always the high "
        },
        {
          "text": "e",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 3's rule tracked the third through all three inversions. Here is the mirror version, tracking the fifth instead — and it turns out to depend on the same one thing, the inversion, never the string set."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": [
