    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Start from a shape already under your fingers. C minor root position on strings 1-2-3 is "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 5 4 3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". The fifth is the top note there — drop it one more fret and the chord turns diminished: "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 5 4 2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "C4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gb4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "triad-shape",
      "props": {
        "root": "C",
        "quality": "diminished",
        "strings": "1-2-3",
        "inversion": "root",
        "caption": "C diminished, root position, strings 1-2-3."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Read it from major instead, if that's the shape more under your fingers: C major root position is "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 5 5 3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Two fingers move — the middle note and the top note — each one fret, and the chord goes from major to diminished in a single step."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One spelling to fix: the flat fifth of "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", not "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Same fret, different name — the chord stacks thirds off "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", so it needs a letter a third above "
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and that letter is "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Build a triad on every note of the C major scale and exactly one of them comes out diminished: "
        },
        {
          "text": "B diminished",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". It is the only diminished triad whose three notes are all inside C major, and no augmented triad fits inside a major scale at all."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "There's a reason this chord shows up where it does. "
        },
        {
          "text": "G7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Take the "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " away and what's left is "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — a "
        },
        {
          "text": "B diminished",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " triad. That explains how it behaves: it holds the two notes doing the leaning, "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a semitone under "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a semitone above "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", without the root that would let it land."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Don't mistake this for a drop-in replacement for "
        },
        {
          "text": "G7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". It has none of the bass leap that makes a dominant land as an ending, so it's common as a connecting chord mid-phrase and rare as a full stop. (Seventh chords are out of scope here — "
        },
        {
          "text": "G7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is named as a chord you already play, not taught here.)"
        }
      ]
    },
