          "text": "F4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", degrees "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 b5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". From there, C major root position, "
        },
        {
          "text": "x x x 5 5 3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", is one fret up on string 3 and two frets up on the other two. The hand doesn't travel; it closes up, from a three-fret spread to a two-fret one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "First inversion is the one you'll meet more often, and there's a reason. In root position the flat fifth sits between the bass and an upper voice — the roughest place for it. Move to first inversion and the bass becomes the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " instead, so the rough interval moves up between two upper voices, out of the bass entirely."
        }
      ]
    },
